# Exhaustiveness: the summary block must be EXACTLY this. The branch adds one
# template to a single-app chart, so one app, one added resource, nothing
# modified - any extra app section or resource fails here rather than being
# quietly pinned by the next --regenerate.
expect:affected=1 changed=1 resources=+1,-0,~0
# THE PAIRING THIS CASE EXISTS FOR. The policy directory exists only on the
# branch, so the base side has none and is not linted, while the target side is.
# Both halves must be in the report together: the finding, and the note that
# explains why it is one-sided.
#
# Without the note, a reader cannot tell an unlinted side from a clean one, and the
# label contract reads a [target]-only finding as "introduced by this change" - which
# is exactly how a PRE-EXISTING violation comes to look new once a first policy
# starts detecting it. The note is the only thing in the report that says the two
# sides are not comparable for this linter.
#
# SCOPE, so this case is not read as more than it is: its branch adds the violating
# Deployment as well as the policy, so THIS finding really was introduced by the
# change. That is not a weaker fixture but the only one available - a change touching
# only policies/ selects no application at all (nothing under any source path
# changed), so argocdf exits before rendering and no linter runs. The Deployment does
# double duty: it is the violation AND what makes cluster-info affected. What the
# case pins is therefore the MECHANISM - one side skipped, the note emitted, the
# labels one-sided - not the pre-existing-violation story that mechanism protects.
must:\[base\] \[lint-kyverno#1\] not linted: no policies in "policies/kyverno-added"
must:\[target\] \[lint-kyverno#1/require-pinned-images\] Deployment/cluster-info-web
# The note must be one-sided too, or it proves nothing: on target the policies ARE
# there and kyverno really ran, which is what makes the base-side note meaningful
# rather than a line argocdf prints for every side.
must-not:\[target\] \[lint-kyverno#1\] not linted
# A side that was never linted cannot have findings. A [base] finding here would
# mean the skip did not happen and the case is testing nothing.
must-not:\[base\] \[lint-kyverno#1/
# The violation must actually reach the render - a finding without it would mean
# kyverno linted something other than this diff.
must:^\+.*image: nginx:latest
